Patient transfer and manual handling injuries
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Sudden onset musculoskeletal pain, Onset during transfer manoeuvre, Localized muscle spasm, Absence of neurological deficits

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-036
Reason for Visit: Acute severe lumbar pain restricting all mobility.
Location: Group residential home
Present at Visit: Patient (Support Worker) and Facility Manager
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a support worker who experienced sudden, agonizing lower back pain while performing a manual transfer of a resident 2 hours ago. The patient is currently incapacitated on the floor, unable to bear weight or mobilize to a vehicle for ED transfer. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the necessity of immediate, on-site clinical evaluation to rule out acute spinal cord compression or major structural failure before safe mobilization could be attempted.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I felt a huge pop in my lower back lifting the resident, and now my muscles are locked up so tight I cannot stand or crawl.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 2-hour history of sudden onset musculoskeletal pain in the lumbar spine. The pain began immediately following a physical lifting and repositioning manoeuvre of a heavy, non-weight-bearing resident. The patient describes a distinct sensation of a "pop" or tearing during the exertion, followed instantly by severe, localized muscle spasm and guarding. The pain is exacerbated severely by any attempt at movement and is only slightly relieved by remaining completely still in a supine position. The patient denies any radicular symptoms (no shooting pain down the legs, no numbness, normal bowel/bladder control).
Functional Status: Normally fully independent; currently completely incapacitated and floor-bound.
Social/Safety Concerns: Occupational injury requiring formal documentation; patient is stranded on the floor of the workplace.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80.0 Kg
General: Patient is lying supine on the floor, visibly grimacing, diaphoretic, and extremely guarded against any movement.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals severe, board-like localized muscle spasm of the bilateral lumbar paraspinal muscles. There is exquisite soft tissue tenderness on palpation over the L4-S1 paraspinal region, but no midline bony step-off or exquisite spinous process tenderness. Active and passive range of motion of the lumbar spine is virtually zero due to pain. Neurological exam of the lower extremities is intact: straight leg raise is negative bilaterally (no radiculopathy), reflexes are 2+ and symmetric, and sensation to light touch is preserved, confirming an absence of neurological deficits.
Environmental Observations: Manual handling equipment (hoist) is visible in the corner but was reportedly not used for this specific transfer.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. No analgesia taken yet due to inability to access medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Patient transfer and manual handling injuries (Acute severe lumbar strain/sprain) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Acute Lumbar Disc Herniation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the complete absence of radicular neurological deficits (negative straight leg raise, normal sensation/reflexes).

Lumbar Vertebral Compression Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely as the pain is primarily in the soft tissue paraspinals rather than localized to the bony spinous processes, and there is a clear mechanism of muscular exertion rather than axial loading/trauma.

Nephrolithiasis (Renal Colic) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the mechanical mechanism of injury, the relief with rest, and the absence of colicky, radiating groin pain or hematuria.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Once mobilized)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe incapacitating lumbar pain episode directly following a high-risk manual handling task. The objective findings of profound paraspinal muscle spasm and tachycardia driven by pain, combined with a strictly normal neurological exam, confirmed a severe acute mechanical soft-tissue injury rather than a surgical spinal emergency. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because the patient was physically stranded on the floor in severe distress. Immediate, in-person clinical clearance and administration of acute parenteral or rapidly acting oral analgesia were mandatory to break the spasm cycle and allow safe mobilization without causing secondary injury.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered an intramuscular injection of Ketorolac 30mg to provide rapid, potent anti-inflammatory analgesia. Guided the patient through a slow, assisted log-roll and mobilization to a chair once the spasm partially subsided. Provided a formal medical certificate for a workplace injury claim.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you consistently use the mechanical hoist for every transfer regardless of perceived time constraints, you eliminate the axial load on your spine; therefore, committing to a "zero manual lift" policy protects your career and livelihood. When you perform 5 minutes of targeted core-activation exercises before starting a shift, you brace your spine; making this a daily pre-work habit significantly reduces the risk of recurrent strains.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to occupational physiotherapy for acute rehabilitation.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended a supportive lumbar brace for the first 48 hours of recovery.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to assess functional recovery and manage return-to-work planning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop sudden weakness in your legs, numbness in your groin/saddle area, or lose control of your bowel or bladder.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of soft tissue strain is clinical; imaging is generally not indicated initially unless "red flag" neurological symptoms develop.
Clinical Course: The acute, severe spasm will likely persist for 48-72 hours before gradually receding. Full functional recovery typically takes 2-4 weeks with appropriate physiotherapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Strictly avoid prolonged bed rest after today, as this worsens stiffness. Alternate 20 minutes of gentle walking with resting. Seek urgent care if shooting pain develops down the back of your leg.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The heavy lifting caused micro-tears in the thick muscles supporting your spine. Your body reacted by locking those muscles in a severe spasm to act as a splint to prevent further movement. The injection forcefully reduces the inflammation to break the spasm cycle so you can stand up.
Actionable Steps: Apply heat packs to the lower back for 20 minutes every 2 hours. Alternate between short walks and resting. Engage with occupational physiotherapy immediately.

